  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nCurrently, satellite and radar show mostly cloudy skies with \nscattered light rain/virga moving across northern lower and eastern \nupper. Very little rain has reached the ground over the last 24 \nhours, with a couple hundredths near the tip of the mitt and over \neastern upper with up to 2 tenths near Whitefish point. The 00z KAPX \nRAOB shows 1 inch PWATs struggling to overcome the dry air in the \nlower levels, with low level RH at 69%. Misaligned forcing has \nresulted in its weakening trend which is continuing into this \nmorning. Brief light rain will be possible into this morning for a \nspot or two, however it will mostly continue to be clouds. A cooler \nand drier airmass near the surface will continue to seep into MI \ntoday, while more ideal upper forcing exits to the east. Skies will \ntrend towards partly sunny for most spots (besides M-72 and south) \nthis afternoon under breezy north winds. High temperatures will be \n10 to almost 20 degrees cooler today, reaching into the 40s and 50s.\n\nThe 1033 mb surface high will slide over Ontario CAN tonight, \nturning low level winds east. Increasing cloud cover from activity \nover the plains and a 3 to 4 mb W/E sfc gradient across the northern \npart of the state will make fog formation difficult tonight. Under \nthis cooler airmass, temperatures will drop into low 30s for eastern \nupper and interior cold spots of northern lower MI. The heat and \nmoisture differences from the air and the inland & Great Lakes will \nbe high, resulting in a moisture flux.. so localized fog and mist \nwill remain possible, but low confidence for widespread fog exists \ntonight. \n\nTuesday, a shortwave and surface cyclone will continue to intensify \nand move towards the northern plains up an over a building upper \nridge over the Ohio River valley. Southwest winds in the lower \nlevels return with east winds near the surface persisting. \nTemperatures will warm but slightly due to the continued influence \nof the surface high and cloud cover. Very low chances for \nprecipitation in the area, however slight chances exist for far \nwestern locations. MI will be on the fringes of the warm sector as \nthe cyclone reaches CAN late Tuesday, however by Wednesday the \nsurface high to the east is far enough away that winds become more \ninfluence by continued WAA to the west. Winds will turn south with \ntemperatures warming back into the 60s. For late October, these high \ntemperatures will start to approach records near northern lower. \n\nA stronger Pacific system will have reached the northern plains mid \nweek, which not only is enhancing the WAA over the western Great \nLakes region but bringing deep subtropical moisture northward and \nproviding the opportunity for better forcing. Widespread light rain \nchances with embedded showers along a passing cold front will move \nthrough Thursday. At this time, chances remain low for this to bring \nimpactful weather. NBM probabilities show a 30% to 40% chance of NW \nlower and eastern upper seeing a greater than a tenth of an inch of \nrain. Higher amounts are possible due to the possibility of the \nconvective element near the boundary. Drier air will cut off \nprecipitation chances for the weekend, with temperatures remaining \nmild.",
